WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.069
You're now ready to define the first agent in your system,

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.238
the User Intent Agent.

3
00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.440
Its main goal is to help you brainstorm ideas

4
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.943
for the type of Knowledge Graph you can construct

5
00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:13.046
and what questions you'd like to answer from the graph.

6
00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:15.048
Let's get coding.

7
00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.549
If you think back to the

8
00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.251
diagram that we'd seen in lesson two

9
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:21.054
that had the overall Knowledge Graph Agent architecture,

10
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:24.557
we're going to be working through the Structured Data Agent,

11
00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.060
which is the sub-agent here that takes care of

12
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.262
taking information from CSV files,

13
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:33.566
taking that data and transforming that
into a graph ultimately.

14
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.869
Now we're going to do that progressively,

15
00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.871
we're going to do that in this elaborative way.

16
00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.672
And the first step is to

17
00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.574
actually understanding what is the user intent.

18
00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.977
What happens here in this first phase

19
00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.579
when we're establishing what is the user's goal

20
00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.782
and a description of what they're trying to achieve

21
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.984
will influence everything else that the agents do.

22
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.953
This sets the direction overall
for what we're trying to achieve

23
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.256
and all the agents will respond to that appropriately.

24
00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:00.560
So, this particular agent has got one main job.

25
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.962
The output of it is saving to the memory

26
00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:04.964
what is the approved user goal.

27
00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:08.001
And to do that, it's got two tools to work with.

28
00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:09.702
It has one tool where it

29
00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.504
first perceives what is the user's goal

30
00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:15.975
based on some interaction during
the conversation it's having with the user.

31
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.978
And then based on what it understands
the user wants,

32
00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.014
it's only able to say, okay,

33
00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:22.982
here's what I perceive that you want.

34
00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.985
And so it's going to call a tool
for actually capturing that.

35
00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:28.188
And then it'll tell the user,

36
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.756
Here's what I think you want.

37
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.425
And then the user has the option of saying,
yes, that's right

38
00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.761
or no, that's not quite right,
try again.

39
00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.664
If you want to try again, then the agent's

40
00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:40.200
going to have to continue to use
this set_perceived_user_goal

41
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.970
until the user thinks,
actually that's the correct thing. I approve that.

42
00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.873
And only when the user has approved,

43
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.375
the agent should then call
this approve_perceived_user_goal.

44
00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.411
And we'll see how these tools work.

45
00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.646
kind of in collaboration to update the memory

46
00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.249
and actually capture this approved_user_goal.

47
00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:59.652
So notice, I think critically,

48
00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:03.456
set_perceived_user_goal cannot actually itself
set the approved_user_goal.

49
00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.425
It's only by calling this tool here.

50
00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.627
This tool ends up being a guard

51
00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.629
ensuring that we have a checkpoint with the user,

52
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.232
that the user is actually themselves explicitly said,

53
00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.234
yes, this is good, let's go with it.

54
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.870
Okay, with the agent details in mind,

55
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.805
we'll go ahead and do the usual

56
00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.473
setup and import libraries that we need.

57
00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.878
And we'll set up the LLM

58
00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:26.446
and a quick sanity check.

59
00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:31.851
Let's see, how did it respond this time?
Kind of consistently.

60
00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.154
Yes, I'm ready. How can I assist you today?

61
00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:39.626
So we can start to define the
User Intent Agent itself.

62
00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:43.029
And we're going to start by actually
piece by piece describing

63
00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.964
what is the prompt that we actually want to give,

64
00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.599
what are the instructions for this agent.

65
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.601
The first part of the prompt

66
00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.336
that you would want to define is

67
00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.938
what is this agent's role

68
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.940
and also what is its goal.

69
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.642
So here we're going to say

70
00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:59.646
this agent is an expert at
knowledge graph use cases.

71
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.749
Your primary goal is to help the user

72
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.450
come up with a knowledge graph use case.

73
00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.153
This is basically saying your job, dear agent,

74
00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:09.856
is to help the user ideate on some ideas,

75
00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.791
what is it trying to accomplish,

76
00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.560
what is the user trying to accomplish.

77
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.062
The agent's goal is to help the user

78
00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.764
figure out what their goal is.

79
00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.934
The next part of the prompt we're going to add

80
00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:23.836
is what I'm calling conversational hints.

81
00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.338
This is helping the agent understand within

82
00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:27.040
the context of the role that it has,

83
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.841
the goal that it's trying to achieve,

84
00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.643
how should it go about its business.

85
00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.012
So here, because we're in the context

86
00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.048
of figuring out what the user's intent is,

87
00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:37.050
trying to work with the user on ideation,

88
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.952
we have some basic ideas about

89
00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:40.687
well, if the user isn't sure what

90
00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.455
to do, you can make some suggestions,

91
00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.158
particularly around classic use cases for knowledge graphs

92
00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:46.859
because of course, this agent is

93
00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.861
an expert at knowledge graph use cases.

94
00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.633
Now, perhaps LLMs already know a lot about
knowledge graph use cases,

95
00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.702
but if you're doing this for your

96
00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:57.437
own internal production system in some enterprise,

97
00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.839
LLMs may not know anything about your business.

98
00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.474
So this is a great opportunity

99
00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.343
for you to explain to the agent

100
00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.078
what it is that goes on in your

101
00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.646
business and what matters to you and what doesn't.

102
00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:10.650
So here, I explain a couple different use cases
for knowledge graphs.

103
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.152
You can use them for social networks, logistics,

104
00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.355
recommendation systems, fraud detection,

105
00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:17.657
of course things like pop culture if you

106
00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.659
want to keep track of movies, books, or music.

107
00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:23.863
Because it's so important for setting

108
00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:25.765
the overall direction of the multi-agent system,

109
00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:28.635
I doubled down on like what is it

110
00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:31.004
that a user goal actually is composed of.

111
00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.306
And here I described within the prompt

112
00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:35.008
that a user goal has two components.

113
00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.577
One is the kind of graph.

114
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:40.580
So are you creating a social graph, are

115
00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.216
you creating a logistics graph,
what could it be?

116
00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:45.952
So the kind of graph is here,

117
00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.688
described to the agent as being

118
00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:50.890
at most three words that describe

119
00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.458
what is the graph that we're creating.

120
00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.361
And in addition to the kind of graph
that you're creating,

121
00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:58.364
there's also the component
of describe that graph.

122
00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:00.566
So provide a few sentences

123
00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.670
about the intention for that graph.
So, for example,

124
00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.873
If we had said "USA freight logistics",

125
00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:10.543
the description could be a dynamic routing
and delivery system

126
00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:15.743
for cargo. This is by way of doing some few-shot learning,

127
00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:19.552
really emphasizing to the agent
and through the agent to an LLM

128
00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:21.554
what it is you're trying to achieve.

129
00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.956
Because this is so important, it's worth

130
00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.059
repeating both here in the prompt itself

131
00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.361
and then later in the tool descriptions that

132
00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:30.563
are actually going to use the user goal,

133
00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:32.799
both in defining what it is and

134
00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:34.867
also then approving what has been understood.

135
00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:38.071
The last part of the prompt
that we're going to put together

136
00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:39.839
is the chain of thought directions.

137
00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:43.042
Now, chain of thought can be as simple as, hey,

138
00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:45.345
think carefully, think step by step.

139
00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.714
But here, we're going to be
a bit more particular.

140
00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:50.216
We have some steps that we want the

141
00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:52.318
LLM to go through, so we're going to

142
00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.153
say very specifically, here's what I want

143
00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.189
you to do one step at a time.

144
00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:58.291
And the agent might do variations of this,

145
00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:00.360
but by being very specific here when we

146
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.261
know what we'd like the agent to do,

147
00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:04.664
it very much helps focus the agent's attention

148
00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.766
to figure out how it should proceed

149
00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:08.968
given a user's initial interactions.

150
00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:12.805
So, most importantly,
it needs to understand the user's goal.

151
00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:17.310
Again, we're going to reiterate,
that's the kind_of_graph along with the description.

152
00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.312
And if it's not sure,

153
00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:21.914
that the agent should ask
clarifying questions as needed.

154
00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:26.018
And then only when the agent thinks it understands
the user's goal,

155
00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.888
should it call the set_perceived_user_goal tool.

156
00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:31.190
That's what's actually going to record

157
00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.393
into memory the user goal as understood

158
00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:36.996
with the two components of kind_of_graph
along with the description.

159
00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:41.334
Finally, present that perceived
user goal to the user,

160
00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:43.436
asking them for confirmation.

161
00:06:43.436 --> 00:06:45.838
Here's what I think you just said,
am I right?

162
00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:48.641
If the user agrees, only then

163
00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:51.544
can the agent call the
approve_perceived_user_goal tool.

164
00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:55.148
Here we give lots of extra instructions
about calling this tool,

165
00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:58.117
so that the agent really understands
what this tool is doing.

166
00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.520
That upon calling this tool,

167
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.322
that the current perceived goal will be

168
00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:06.125
saved into state under the approved_user_goal key.

169
00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:10.396
Okay. All of these parts together will form the prompt

170
00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.598
that we're going to give to the agent.

171
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.803
To combine this together,
we'll use some Python string templates,

172
00:07:17.003 --> 00:07:18.905
and we're just go ahead and actually

173
00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:20.807
insert the variables that we defined earlier,

174
00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.810
the agent_role_and_goal, the agent_conversational_hints,

175
00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:27.113
the agent_output_definition,
and then also the chain-of-thought directions.

176
00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:29.449
We'll go ahead and print that to the screen

177
00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.150
so you can see what the total thing looks like.

178
00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:38.858
You can now go ahead and define those tools.

179
00:07:41.194 --> 00:07:43.830
The first tool is to set_perceived_user_goal.

180
00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:45.998
And you can see in the comments here,

181
00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.900
this is to encourage collaboration with the user

182
00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.102
by setting this up inside of a tool

183
00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.904
and by having only through that tool

184
00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:55.208
saving the values that it perceives into memory,

185
00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:57.310
we really help the agent focus.

186
00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.179
is on like what does it mean
to understand the user's goal

187
00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.881
because it has to call this tool,

188
00:08:01.881 --> 00:08:03.649
it knows that it has these two

189
00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:05.384
components. We said that in the prompt

190
00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:07.386
and then here in the tool definition itself,

191
00:08:07.386 --> 00:08:09.188
it can see that there's two arguments.

192
00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:10.890
It has to pass in the kind

193
00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.592
of graph and also a graph description.

194
00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:14.494
And also, you'll notice

195
00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:17.997
We have the tool_context passed in
as the last parameter.

196
00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.765
If you remember from the previous lesson,

197
00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:21.968
when the last argument is a ToolContext,

198
00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:25.338
ADK will automatically inject that
when this tool is being called.

199
00:08:25.872 --> 00:08:29.742
in the tool description,
we reiterate what this is doing

200
00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:31.644
that it's saving the perceived user's goal,

201
00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:34.847
including the kind of graph along
with a description of it.

202
00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.650
And we also describe what the arguments are.

203
00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:40.653
These should be exactly
the same as what we said to

204
00:08:40.653 --> 00:08:42.355
the agent inside of the instructions earlier

205
00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:44.056
that will be part of the prompt.

206
00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.059
Now the tool definition itself is going to say it again.

207
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:48.761
The more times you can say

208
00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:50.263
it, the less likely that the LLM

209
00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:52.532
it's going to do the wrong thing
when calling the tool.

210
00:08:52.632 --> 00:08:54.634
And inside of the tool, it's pretty simple.

211
00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:57.036
All it's going to do is assemble

212
00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:59.338
a small dictionary that is the user_goal_data

213
00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:02.441
that is composed of those two components,
the kind_of_graph

214
00:09:02.542 --> 00:09:05.645
along with the graph_description,
just as passed into the tool.

215
00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:08.247
So this is really just a very simple

216
00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.449
way of actually encapsulating
how to access memory,

217
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.852
but also focusing the access to memory.

218
00:09:13.286 --> 00:09:16.088
So the context state is updated
with that dictionary

219
00:09:16.489 --> 00:09:18.090
because this has been an update.

220
00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:21.294
The ADK will see that
there's been a change to state.

221
00:09:21.294 --> 00:09:24.797
It'll propagate that delta to anybody else
who needs to perceive it

222
00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:27.099
I'm inside of the runtime environment.

223
00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:32.104
You can then define the next tool, which

224
00:09:32.104 --> 00:09:34.173
is used to approve that perceived user goal.

225
00:09:34.574 --> 00:09:36.809
Now, this should only get triggered after

226
00:09:36.809 --> 00:09:39.078
the perceived user goal has been set,

227
00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:42.081
and then the user has also said, yes, I approve that.

228
00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:45.184
If both of those things are true, this tool should be called.

229
00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:49.355
Now, when you can, whenever it's possible and sensible,

230
00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:53.059
inside of a tool, it's worth trusting
but verifying that the LLM

231
00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:55.628
is doing the right thing.

232
00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.063
In this tool, it should only

233
00:09:57.063 --> 00:09:58.731
be called upon approval from the user.

234
00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:01.534
So we're going to iterate again with the LLM,

235
00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:04.637
hey, only call this after the user has approved.

236
00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:07.540
And then if the user has approved by calling this,

237
00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.875
the tool will record the perceived

238
00:10:09.875 --> 00:10:12.044
user goal as the approved user goal.

239
00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:14.347
And again, we're going to say to the agent,

240
00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:16.182
only call this tool if the user

241
00:10:16.182 --> 00:10:18.050
has explicitly approved the perceived user goal.

242
00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:22.021
inside of the tool to check a little bit.

243
00:10:22.321 --> 00:10:25.324
Because we require that
the perceived user goal has already been set,

244
00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:26.926
we can go ahead and check that that

245
00:10:26.926 --> 00:10:28.527
has been set before we do anything else.

246
00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:31.530
Now, how we handle this setting is really important

247
00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:33.766
because the call to the tool can

248
00:10:33.766 --> 00:10:35.534
result either successfully or in an error.

249
00:10:36.035 --> 00:10:37.536
So here we have a very

250
00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:39.338
specific error that we might turn back.

251
00:10:39.572 --> 00:10:42.675
If the PERCEIVED_USER_GOAL isn't in the current context.

252
00:10:42.675 --> 00:10:45.378
If it's not in the tool_context.state,

253
00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:47.079
so the agent's memory.

254
00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:49.081
If the PERCEIVED_USER_GOAL is not there,

255
00:10:49.081 --> 00:10:51.384
we're going to go ahead
and return an error from this tool.

256
00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:54.387
And the error message that we're going to supply

257
00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:56.389
is meant to actually help the LLM understand,

258
00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:58.391
here's what what went wrong,

259
00:10:58.391 --> 00:11:00.493
here's what you should do to actually fix the problem.

260
00:11:00.693 --> 00:11:02.328
So we're telling the LLM that the

261
00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:03.996
perceived user goal has not been set

262
00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.033
and that it should set the perceived user goal first

263
00:11:07.033 --> 00:11:09.235
or ask clarifying questions if it's unsure

264
00:11:09.235 --> 00:11:11.737
what the user's intentions are.

265
00:11:12.238 --> 00:11:14.240
So this is really reiterating to the agent

266
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.742
in all these different places from the prompt,

267
00:11:16.742 --> 00:11:19.045
to the definition of the tools,

268
00:11:19.045 --> 00:11:21.047
to also the error messages that come back from the tools,

269
00:11:21.247 --> 00:11:23.249
encouraging the LLM to do the right thing

270
00:11:23.249 --> 00:11:25.051
based on what's happening.

271
00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:28.888
If it was successful, all we do then is copy the state

272
00:11:28.888 --> 00:11:32.291
from the perceived user goal into the approved user goal.

273
00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:34.794
So notice the only way for this to happen,

274
00:11:34.794 --> 00:11:38.464
since we're not passing an argument
into the approve_perceived_user_goal,

275
00:11:38.764 --> 00:11:41.167
The approved user goal only gets set if there

276
00:11:41.167 --> 00:11:43.569
is a perceived user goal. It gets copied in,

277
00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:45.337
and then both the perceived and approved

278
00:11:45.337 --> 00:11:47.373
user goals should be available inside of state.

279
00:11:48.307 --> 00:11:51.243
For convenience, we'll add both of those to a list

280
00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:52.812
because we know we're going to pass in

281
00:11:52.812 --> 00:11:54.613
a list when we create the actual agent itself.

282
00:11:55.481 --> 00:11:59.985
So we've got our two tools,
setting perceived_user_goal and approving the perceived_user_goal.

283
00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.289
So, you can define the user intent agent.

284
00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:05.491
You're going to give it a name with

285
00:12:05.491 --> 00:12:07.493
a version that's going to be unique version name.

286
00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.596
And we're just going to use the LLM that we defined earlier.

